An experiment that makes short strings immortal, and it LEAKS BY DESIGN.

OFF UNLESS CIDER_IMMORTAL_SHORT_STRINGS NAMES A MAXIMUM LENGTH. It retains every string at or under
that length once more at creation, which never frees them, including duplicates. It is not a fix
and must not become one.

WHAT IT IS FOR. On macOS a short ASCII string is a TAGGED POINTER: no allocation, nothing to
deallocate, immortal. Applications rely on that without knowing they do. Swift Publisher hands six
character strings to -addObserver:forKeyPath:options:context: without retaining them, which is safe
on a Mac and fatal here, because we allocate a real object and the pool frees it.

Implementing tagged pointers properly touches pointer encoding, the objc runtime and every place
that assumes a pointer is dereferenceable. Before anyone spends that, this answers the only
question that matters: is the diagnosis right. Same scenario, same build, one variable:

    OFF   exit=139 SIGSEGV, capture after the click 2639 bytes, fully black
    ON    no crash, capture after the click 107570 bytes, LOOKED AT: the document editor intact,
          title bar, menu bar, toolbar, page thumbnails, rulers, text and inspector

So the cause was removed and the effect went with it. The real fix is then a choice between tagged
pointer strings, which is what macOS does, and immortal short immutable strings, which buys the
same safety for far less risk. The memory cost of the second has to be measured first, and this
switch is an UPPER BOUND on it rather than the cost itself, because it retains duplicates too.

Task #249.
--- a/CFString.c
+++ b/CFString.c
@@ -1551,6 +1551,35 @@
     }
 #endif
 
+    /*
+     * AN EXPERIMENT, NOT A FIX, AND IT LEAKS BY DESIGN. Task #249.
+     *
+     * On macOS a short ASCII string is a TAGGED POINTER: no allocation, nothing to deallocate,
+     * immortal. Applications rely on that without knowing it. Swift Publisher hands six character
+     * strings to -addObserver:forKeyPath:options:context: without retaining them, which is safe
+     * there and fatal here, because we allocate a real object that the pool then frees.
+     *
+     * Retaining every short string once more makes them immortal the crude way. It proves or
+     * disproves the whole diagnosis in one run WITHOUT building tagged pointers, which touches
+     * pointer encoding, the objc runtime and every place that assumes a pointer is
+     * dereferenceable. If the crash survives this, the diagnosis is wrong and no one should spend
+     * a week on tagged pointers.
+     *
+     * OFF unless CIDER_IMMORTAL_SHORT_STRINGS names a maximum length, because it is a deliberate
+     * leak of every short string the process ever makes.
+     */
+    if (str != NULL) {
+        static int limit = -1;
+
+        if (limit < 0) {
+            const char *v = getenv("CIDER_IMMORTAL_SHORT_STRINGS");
+
+            limit = (v != NULL && v[0] != '\0') ? atoi(v) : 0;
+        }
+        if (limit > 0 && __CFStrLength((CFStringRef) str) <= limit)
+            CFRetain(str);
+    }
+
     return str;
 }
 
